{"ts": "2026-09-23T20:23:41", "conversation_id": "20260923T202113-68a1e396", "role": "assistant", "kind": "completion", "content": "```diff\n--- a/frontend/static/style/css/style.css\n+++ b/frontend/static/style/css/style.css\n@@ -49,4 +49,42 @@\n 54. Custom Widgets\n 55. Custom Responsive\n ----------------------------\n */\n+/*\n+--------------------------------\n+    : SMS Provider Abstraction :\n+--------------------------------\n+\n+Common SMS provider inputs (all provider adapters must accept these):\n+\n+- destination: recipient E.164 phone number (customer phone number).\n+- template/message: message text or template reference with variables.\n+- sender id: alphanumeric sender ID or phone number configured with the provider.\n+- status callback: webhook/URL where the provider reports delivery status.\n+- cost/error metadata: per-message cost, provider error code, and retry state.\n+\n+Privacy and opt-in boundaries for customer phone numbers:\n+\n+- Do not send SMS to a customer phone number without an explicit opt-in\n+  for the alert channel. Transactional order alerts are allowed only when\n+  the customer has provided the number and accepted SMS notifications.\n+- Never use customer phone numbers for marketing or unsolicited messages.\n+- Record the opt-in source and timestamp; provide an opt-out mechanism\n+  and honor it immediately.\n+- Phone numbers must be treated as PII: encrypt at rest, restrict access,\n+  and never log full numbers.\n+- Only pass the number to the configured SMS provider for message\n+  delivery; do not share it for any other purpose.\n+\n+Test fixture proposal:\n+\n+- Provide a FakeSmsProvider fixture that records sent messages in memory,\n+  returns a deterministic provider message ID, and can simulate success,\n+  delivery failure, or per-message cost. It must not perform network I/O.\n+\n+Default behavior:\n+\n+- No real SMS is sent by default. Unless an SMS provider is explicitly\n+  configured, the system uses FakeSmsProvider so tests and local\n+  development never send real messages.\n+*/\n /* Mukta Vaani bundled locally - offline-safe */\n```\n\nFILE: api/tests/unit/sms-provider-documentation.test.js\n<<<CONTENT>>>\ndescribe('SMS provider abstraction documentation', () => {\n  it('documents the provider boundary in the frontend style guide', () => {\n    const assert = require('assert');\n    const fs = require('fs');\n    const path = require('path');\n    const styleCss = fs.readFileSync(\n      path.join(__dirname, '../../../frontend/static/style/css/style.css'),\n      'utf8'\n    );\n\n    assert.ok(styleCss.includes('SMS Provider Abstraction'));\n    assert.ok(styleCss.includes('destination'));\n    assert.ok(styleCss.includes('template/message'));\n    assert.ok(styleCss.includes('sender id'));\n    assert.ok(styleCss.includes('status callback'));\n    assert.ok(styleCss.includes('cost/error metadata'));\n    assert.ok(styleCss.includes('opt-in'));\n    assert.ok(styleCss.includes('FakeSmsProvider'));\n    assert.ok(/no real sms is sent by default/i.test(styleCss));\n  });\n});\n<<<END>>>\n\nIssues fixed: Documented the SMS provider abstraction boundary in the frontend style guide comment block: common provider inputs, privacy/opt-in boundaries, fake provider fixture proposal, and explicit statement that no real SMS is sent by default. Added a regression test asserting the documentation exists.\n\nTests/checks run: Not executed in this environment. The added test fails against the previous file content because none of the required strings were present, and passes once the documentation block is added.\n\nRemaining concerns: The documentation lives in a CSS comment because no docs directory was present in the allowed paths; if a real docs home is added later, this content should move there, but the boundary description remains valid.\n\nFinal status: READY", "meta": {"model": "openrouter/auto"}}
